Below is a **step‑by‑step recipe** for adding a hillshade layer that is driven by a **raster‑DEM** source to a MapLibre GL JS map (or to a static style JSON).  
Everything is plain JavaScript – no extra plugins are required – as long as you are using a recent version of MapLibre GL JS (≥ 2.0, which ships the `hillshade` layer type).

---

## 1️⃣  What you need

| Item | Why it matters |
|------|----------------|
| **MapLibre GL JS** | The library that renders the map. |
| **A raster‑DEM source** (e.g. Mapbox Terrain‑RGB, AWS Terrain Tiles, or your own GeoTIFF → XYZ tiles) | The hillshade layer can only read a source of type **`raster-dem`**. |
| **A container element** (`<div id="map"></div>`) | Where the map will be placed. |
| **An access token** (if you use a hosted service) | Some providers (Mapbox, AWS) require a token. |

> **Tip:** If you only want a visual hillshade (no 3‑D terrain), you can skip the `terrain` property of the map. If you also want a 3‑D terrain surface, add a `terrain` source as shown at the end of this answer.

---

## 2️⃣  Minimal HTML + JS example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre GL JS – Hillshade from raster‑DEM</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="initial-scale=1,maximum-scale=1,user-scalable=no" />
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣  Initialise the map
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // ---- Base map (any style you like) ----
        "osm": {
          type: "raster",
          tiles: ["https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png"],
          tileSize: 256,
          attribution: "© OpenStreetMap contributors"
        }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: "osm-basemap",
          type: "raster",
          source: "osm"
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574], // San Francisco
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 0,
    bearing: 0
  });

  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 2️⃣  Add the raster‑DEM source
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // Example using Mapbox Terrain‑RGB (public demo tiles, no token needed for the demo)
  // Replace the URL with your own DEM tiles if you have them.
  map.on('load', () => {
    map.addSource('dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      // The URL pattern must return PNG/TIFF tiles that encode elevation in the
      // Mapbox Terrain‑RGB format (or any 16‑bit DEM that MapLibre can decode).
      // 👉  https://docs.mapbox.com/help/troubleshooting/access-elevation-data/
      url: 'https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/terrain-rgb/{z}/{x}/{y}.png?key=YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY',
      tileSize: 256,
      // Optional: limit maxzoom to the highest zoom you actually have.
      maxzoom: 14,
      attribution: 'Terrain data © <a href="https://www.maptiler.com/">MapTiler</a>'
    });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣  Add the hillshade layer
    // -------------------------------------------------
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hillshade',
      type: 'hillshade',
      source: 'dem',
      // The hillshade layer does **not** need a source‑layer name because a raster‑dem source has only one layer.
      paint: {
        // These are the most common paint properties – tweak them to taste.
        'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.5,               // 0 = flat, 1 = full relief
        'hillshade-illumination-direction': 315,    // Sun direction in degrees clockwise from north
        'hillshade-illumination-anchor': 'map',     // 'map' (fixed) or 'viewport' (moves with camera)
        'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.5)',
        'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,255,255,0.5)',
        'hillshade-accent-color': 'rgba(0,0,0,0.2)'
      }
    });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // (Optional) 4️⃣  Add 3‑D terrain surface
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // If you also want a 3‑D terrain mesh, enable it like this:
    // map.setTerrain({ source: 'dem', exaggeration: 1.5 });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does

| Step | Code snippet | Explanation |
|------|--------------|-------------|
| **1️⃣ Initialise the map** | `new maplibregl.Map({ … })` | Starts a MapLibre map with a simple OSM raster basemap. |
| **2️⃣ Add the DEM source** | `map.addSource('dem', {type:'raster-dem', url:…})` | Declares a source that serves elevation tiles. The source **must** be `type: 'raster-dem'`. |
| **3️⃣ Add the hillshade layer** | `map.addLayer({type:'hillshade', source:'dem', …})` | Creates a visual hillshade that reads the DEM values and renders shadows/highlights. |
| **4️⃣ (Optional) 3‑D terrain** | `map.setTerrain({source:'dem', exaggeration:1.5})` | Gives you a true 3‑D terrain mesh; the hillshade still works on top of it. |

---

## 3️⃣  Adding the same thing via a **style JSON** (no JS)

If you prefer to keep everything in a static style file (e.g. `style.json`), just embed the source and layer definitions:

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "osm": {
      "type": "raster",
      "tiles": ["https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png"],
      "tileSize": 256,
      "attribution": "© OpenStreetMap contributors"
    },
    "dem": {
      "type": "raster-dem",
      "url": "https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/terrain-rgb/{z}/{x}/{y}.png?key=YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY",
      "tileSize": 256,
      "maxzoom": 14,
      "attribution": "Terrain data © MapTiler"
    }
  },
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "osm-basemap",
      "type": "raster",
      "source": "osm"
    },
    {
      "id": "hillshade",
      "type": "hillshade",
      "source": "dem",
      "paint": {
        "hillshade-exaggeration": 0.5,
        "hillshade-illumination-direction": 315,
        "hillshade-shadow-color": "rgba(0,0,0,0.5)",
        "hillshade-highlight-color": "rgba(255,255,255,0.5)",
        "hillshade-accent-color": "rgba(0,0,0,0.2)"
      }
    }
  ],
  "terrain": {
    "source": "dem",
    "exaggeration": 1.5
  }
}
```

Load it with:

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'style.json',
  center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
  zoom: 12
});
```

> **Note:** The `terrain` block is optional – you can keep the style hillshade‑only if you don’t need a 3‑D mesh.

---

## 4️⃣  Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Problem | Why it happens | Fix |
|---------|----------------|-----|
| **Hillshade doesn’t appear** | The source is declared as `raster` instead of `raster-dem`. | Change `type` to `"raster-dem"` and make sure the tiles encode elevation (Mapbox Terrain‑RGB, 16‑bit PNG, etc.). |
| **Tiles return 404** | Wrong URL template or missing API key. | Verify the tile URL works directly in a browser (e.g. `https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/terrain-rgb/12/657/1583.png?key=…`). |
| **Hillshade looks flat** | `hillshade-exaggeration` is `0` (default). | Set a value > 0 (0.2‑1.0 is typical). |
| **Colors look washed out** | Using the default black/white colors on a dark basemap. | Adjust `hillshade-shadow-color`, `hillshade-highlight-color`, and `hillshade-accent-color` to match your basemap palette. |
| **Performance drops on high zoom** | DEM tiles are large (256 px × 256 px with 16‑bit data). | Limit `maxzoom` to the highest resolution you actually need, or use a lower‑resolution DEM for zoom levels > 14. |
| **Hillshade disappears after `setTerrain`** | The terrain source is not the same as the hillshade source. | Use the **same** DEM source for both `terrain` and the hillshade layer, or add a second DEM source if you need different resolutions. |

---

## 5️⃣  Where to get raster‑DEM tiles

| Provider | URL pattern (replace `{z}/{x}/{y}`) | Notes |
|----------|--------------------------------------|-------|
| **Mapbox Terrain‑RGB** (requires token) | `https://api.mapbox.com/v4/mapbox.terrain-rgb/{z}/{x}/{y}@2x.pngraw?access_token=YOUR_TOKEN` | 16‑bit RGB encoding; free tier up to 100 k requests/month. |
| **MapTiler Terrain** (free tier, API key) | `https://api.maptiler.com/tiles/terrain-rgb/{z}/{x}/{y}.png?key=YOUR_KEY` | Same format as Mapbox, easy to use. |
| **AWS Terrain Tiles** (public) | `https://s3.amazonaws.com/elevation-tiles-prod/terrarium/{z}/{x}/{y}.png` | Terrarium format (elevation = R\*256 + G + B/256 – 32768). MapLibre can read it if you set `type: "raster-dem"` and `encoding: "terrarium"` (available from v2.4). |
| **Your own GeoTIFF → XYZ** | Serve via `tileserver-gl`, `tessera`, `tileserver-php`, etc. | Must be pre‑processed to 256 px tiles and optionally re‑projected to Web Mercator. |

> **Quick tip:** If you are experimenting locally, the MapTiler demo key (`YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY`) works for up to 100 k requests per day – perfect for a prototype.

